  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Lead coffins kept turning up jumbled inside a sealed tomb. Christ Church, Barbados. A stone door it took six men to shift.",
        "visual_query": "old stone tomb cemetery",
        "visual_fallback": "stone tomb"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The Chase family vault was opened in 1812, 1816 and 1819. Every time, the heavy coffins were somewhere other than where mourners left them. Who rearranges a tomb nobody can enter?",
        "visual_query": "caribbean churchyard palm trees",
        "visual_fallback": "island churchyard"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 1819 the governor watched masons cement it shut. Sand on the floor, no footprints, no water marks. Opened in 1820: chaos again.",
        "visual_query": "sand floor stone crypt",
        "visual_fallback": "crypt interior"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then the trail goes thin. Andrew Lang went looking in 1907 and found nothing in the parish burial register or the island's newspapers.",
        "visual_query": "old parish register book",
        "visual_fallback": "old ledger"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The whole legend traces to one account printed in 1833 and one unpublished note. Benjamin Radford checked the vault and found the brickwork undamaged.",
        "visual_query": "weathered brick vault wall",
        "visual_fallback": "brick wall"
      }
    ],
    "segment_count": 5,
    "word_count": 123,
